1
00:01:53,350 --> 00:03:49,500
 Nearly 5 billion years ago, more than 10 million centuries, to form the first single cell, the source of all life, flagellates, the first many-celled aquatic plant.

2
00:04:00,900 --> 00:04:20,280
 With the Cambrian period came various new forms of life, medusas, phallobites, ostracoderm, the very first fish.

3
00:04:20,280 --> 00:05:11,960
 4 billion years, 40 million centuries passed before there appeared on solid ground the first amphibian, Ichthyostega.

4
00:05:21,820 --> 00:05:32,580
 In the succeeding millennia, the various species evolved by means of natural selection.

5
00:05:34,230 --> 00:05:38,270
 No sooner had they left the water than reptiles began to colonize the world.

6
00:05:40,770 --> 00:05:50,790
 Thecodont, Archaeopteryx, the first primitive bird, has just taken flight.

7
00:05:54,750 --> 00:05:59,310
 Wegener's theory states that the Earth, one solid mass up until then, underwent division.

8
00:05:59,930 --> 00:06:01,230
 Continents drifted apart.

9
00:06:01,230 --> 00:06:08,780
 The secondary period saw the triumph of the dinosaurs, whose descendants include crocodiles, as well as birds.

10
00:06:09,890 --> 00:06:14,500
 Then nature seems to have tried her hand at some interesting experiments.

11
00:06:15,050 --> 00:06:20,410
 The armored tank model Stegosaurus, 8 meters long, weighing 6 tons, a softy to boot.

12
00:06:20,830 --> 00:06:25,750
 A shame that such an impressive physique was run by a walnut-sized brain and a very tiny head.

13
00:06:28,520 --> 00:06:36,530
 Fearsome Allosaurus, life is hard on softies.

14
00:06:36,530 --> 00:06:42,330
 Our next model is Brontosaurus, 20 meters long, weighing in at 20 tons.

15
00:06:42,490 --> 00:06:47,430
 He too has a tiny head, with a brain no bigger than an apple, not quite enough for 20 tons of muscle.

16
00:06:48,070 --> 00:06:51,350
 Still, he was tops in his category, a heavyweight.

17
00:06:54,450 --> 00:06:58,090
 Even heftier was Diplodocus, at 30 meters, the tallest of the lot.

18
00:06:58,350 --> 00:07:01,310
 He was more streamlined, with a tail like a whip and a swan's neck.

19
00:07:04,640 --> 00:07:11,570
 And here's the all-round champ, the largest beast ever to have walked the Earth.

20
00:07:12,610 --> 00:07:15,630
 Brachiosaurus, 24 meters long, 12 high.

21
00:07:15,630 --> 00:07:18,950
 In relation to his size, his brain was the tiniest.

22
00:07:19,700 --> 00:07:22,880
 This didn't prevent our lizard with arms from reaching the 50-ton mark.

23
00:07:24,440 --> 00:07:32,280
 We have some legendary monsters, Elasmosaurus, not as heavy but still quite presentable at 18 meters.

24
00:07:32,640 --> 00:07:33,920
 Could he be the Loch Ness monster?

25
00:07:34,360 --> 00:07:39,530
 The fabled dragon, Pteranodon, the largest flying reptile with a wingspan of 8 meters.

26
00:07:39,990 --> 00:07:41,810
 Life wasn't easy in those days.

27
00:07:42,590 --> 00:07:43,950
 And how about sea surface?

28
00:07:44,490 --> 00:07:45,740
 Like Tilosaurus.

29
00:07:46,570 --> 00:07:48,390
 Oh, life wasn't easy in those days.

30
00:07:48,390 --> 00:07:50,950
 The duck model, Anatosaurus.

31
00:07:52,100 --> 00:07:55,900
 Though less adventuresome, this plant eater didn't mind an occasional change of diet.

32
00:07:59,360 --> 00:08:04,500
 But, careful, Priceratops doesn't appreciate such interest in his offspring.

33
00:08:10,810 --> 00:08:14,960
 Walking Terror, a real monster, the most ferocious of these giants.

34
00:08:15,220 --> 00:08:21,390
 The King Tyrannosaurus, 15 by 6 meters, 8 tons, jaws lined with a double set of razor-sharp teeth.

35
00:08:43,930 --> 00:08:44,950
 The certain way wasn't easy.

36
00:08:46,100 --> 00:08:52,220
 The dinosaurs, having reigned supreme over the Earth for more than 100 million years, at last gave way to small mammals.

37
00:08:53,390 --> 00:08:58,570
 Unlike the mastodons, these rodents could adapt to climatic conditions and natural upheavals.

38
00:08:58,570 --> 00:09:15,580
 The old of the secondary period gradually faded away.

39
00:09:16,060 --> 00:09:18,700
 From its wreckage, a new world emerged.

40
00:09:27,630 --> 00:09:31,810
 World-sized tree-living insect eaters may have been the forefathers of the primates.

41
00:09:31,850 --> 00:09:37,010
 Those branches would include apes, anthropoids, and hominoids.

42
00:09:43,880 --> 00:09:46,340
 Although actual proof of the connection is hard to come by.

43
00:09:48,480 --> 00:09:49,970
 Here is a rodent.

44
00:09:51,530 --> 00:09:54,050
 Mylodectes, its skull larger, orbits higher.

45
00:09:54,870 --> 00:09:57,190
 Egeoptopithecus, skull still higher, the face flatter.

46
00:09:57,880 --> 00:10:00,000
 Pliopithecus, already an anthropomorphic ape.

47
00:10:03,520 --> 00:10:29,280
 Consul, means, get the food.

48
00:10:37,980 --> 00:10:42,750
 You mean nothing left.

49
00:10:44,280 --> 00:10:48,250
 It seems that male supremacy was open to question, uh, even then.

50
00:10:50,940 --> 00:10:55,300
 One member of the group has, uh, some trouble keeping up.

51
00:10:55,300 --> 00:11:04,460
 Natural selection.

52
00:11:05,630 --> 00:11:24,710
 At work, he's saying, this is my territory, please leave before I lose my temper.

53
00:11:29,340 --> 00:11:40,560
 When food became scarce, they had to leave the forest for the first time and set out into the open country.

54
00:13:02,540 --> 00:13:04,040
 Easier from an upright position.

55
00:13:04,340 --> 00:13:08,700
 As he straightened up, Proconsul's pelvis became stronger to support his trunk, arms, and head.

56
00:13:09,260 --> 00:13:10,420
 His legs grew longer.

57
00:13:13,010 --> 00:13:16,150
 Thousands of years went by, and the great anthropomorphic apes evolved.

58
00:13:16,410 --> 00:13:17,770
 Caniopithecus, 1 meter 20.

59
00:13:18,750 --> 00:13:20,190
 Ramapithecus, 1 meter 30.

60
00:13:21,280 --> 00:13:49,310
 Australopithecus africanus. A meter and a half. Australopithecus robustus. Australopithecus evolved becoming more agile. He was taller due to straighter posture. His brain capacity grew from 450 to 660 cubic centimeters. Here's the Old Duvet Gorge in Tanzania in the heart of Africa, where he lived. In his paradise, many species of animals flourished.

61
00:14:04,540 --> 00:14:07,590
 Obustus was a vegetarian.

62
00:14:21,900 --> 00:14:33,160
 Not that its usefulness had occurred to him yet.

63
00:14:45,100 --> 00:14:51,160
 A million years ago, a tribe of Homo habilis, they were carnivorous.

64
00:15:29,760 --> 00:16:10,300
 Catch nothing, no, you not eat rats.

65
00:16:10,320 --> 00:16:12,480
 Meat, meat, meat, meat, meat, meat, meat.

66
00:16:12,820 --> 00:16:19,400
 You're gonna at least try being polite to us when we ask for something to eat, my friend and me.

67
00:16:32,000 --> 00:16:44,930
 Hey, hey, hey, over here!

68
00:16:53,530 --> 00:16:57,080
 Obustus was hunted to extinction.

69
00:16:58,650 --> 00:17:00,920
 Homo habilis now reigned alone.

70
00:17:00,920 --> 00:17:17,430
 In time, Homo habilis left the grasslands and wandered over other continents, far from his homeland.

71
00:17:19,500 --> 00:17:22,930
 The level of the seas dropped sharply during the glacial periods.

72
00:17:23,830 --> 00:17:28,090
 The sea fell to 100 meters below its modern level, forming land bridges that linked the continents.

73
00:17:34,330 --> 00:17:44,110
 700,000 years before our time, Pithecanthropus, or Ape Man, more correctly called Homo erectus,

74
00:17:44,730 --> 00:17:46,900
 he measured up to 1 meter 60 centimeters.

75
00:17:47,160 --> 00:17:49,900
 His brain volume reached 950 cubic centimeters.

76
00:17:54,900 --> 00:18:00,720
 They're still quite elementary.

77
00:18:00,720 --> 00:18:13,470
 Got it, territory, how does animal belong? Us!

78
